  "query_preview": "..\n\nSE MI continues to reside under the western edge of Northeastern \nhigh pressure. Upper ridging folding into lower MI supports a couple \ndegree warmer day as highs reach the upper 60s. With light \nsouth/southeast winds overnight, fog potential is favored along the \nLakes St Clair-Erie as lake moisture is drawn inland though patchy \nradiative fog also possible further north like the previous couple \nmornings. \n\nUpper ridging is flattened overtop the state Sunday by a cold front \nsagging out of northern Ontario. Core of the accompanying airmass \n(850mb temps 10-11C) allows highs to make a run to 70. Said front \neventually sinks across the CWA early Monday to little fanfare given \nthe detachment from the parent wave over eastern Canada and frontal \norientation parallel to mean flow. Fropa does bring a brief cool \ndown closer to late October averages as 850mb temps fall to low \nsingle digits C with highs in the 50s-low 60s. \n\nArrival of a series of waves over the Pacific Northwest induces low \namplitude ridging over the central CONUS mid to late week, only \nbriefly interrupted by a southern stream low lifting through the \nMidwest. Locally this leads to another stretch of above normal \ntemperatures and limited chances for rain to close out the month.",
